    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 111728\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n928 AM PST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Quiet and pleasant conditions anticipated through Friday morning.\n\n* Cooler conditions and a chance of showers returns late Friday\n  into Saturday, with another weather system poised to affect the\n  region early next week. \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through next Wednesday.\n\nHigh pressure ridging dominates much of the Western US early this\nmorning, with clear skies across the Mojave Desert and Great Basin\nregion. However, changes are lurking over the eastern Pacific as a\nlarge trough sits across the southern Gulf of Alaska. This will\nsend an initial shortwave eastward that will close off and slide\nsouth of our region on Friday, followed by a secondary stronger\nshortwave that will swing through NorCal and the Great Basin\nFriday night into Saturday. \n\nThis secondary wave will feature a fairly sharp decrease in \nheights and direct a healthy moisture plume into the central and \nsouthern Sierra. Forecast trends have become more favorable for \nmoderate to locally heavy snow totals in the Sierra with this \nwave, mainly above 7000 feet, where potential for 5-10 inches of \nsnow exists in the Aspendell area. Went ahead and issued a winter \nstorm watch to account for the increased confidence in winter \nimpacts. Further east, precipitation chances will spread east of \nthe Sierra Friday night into Saturday morning. Moisture plume will\nbe moving fairly swiftly and eaten up considerably by the Sierra,\nhowever, a respectable IVT (Integrated Vapor Transport) plume is \nstill advertised as it moves eastward across the deserts, so \nconfidence is increasing on at least some light rainfall totals \nacross the desert elevations as well. \n\nShortwave ridging will build in Saturday night into Sunday with\ndry conditions resuming, but the storm track will remain active as\nadditional storm systems approach from the eastern Pacific. The\nnext chance for widespread precipitation and gusty winds arrives\nMonday into Tuesday, with additional storm systems possible later\nin the week. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package...\nLight winds persist and follow typical, daily patterns. Chances for \n10+ knots are 10% or less throughout the TAF period. Light to calm \nwinds late this morning become NE at or below 7 kt by early \nafternoon. Expect a wind shift to the SW after 03z with speeds \nremaining light. Similar wind pattern expected tomorrow. VFR \nconditions under clear skies.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Light and diurnal winds \nanticipated across much of the region today and tonight. The \nexception is KIFP where local terrain effects will induce northerly \nwinds of 10-15 knots and gusts around 20-25 knots through sunset. \nVFR conditions persist under clear skies.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Outler\nAVIATION...Moore\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
